Jungle Invasion with Pygame

To view a screenshot of the game and highlight the code more, checkout my google docs pdf - https://docs.google.com/document/d/1DJfDiJGshZd2-0p-9a5qFveIWQ41upzNQvg21SqbNhU/edit?tab=t.0

This project is inspired by Eric Matthes’s book, “Shooting Aliens game” from his Python Crash Course 3rd edition. And though initially aimed to curate something similar through a “Tarzan” lens, was overhauled as more of a top-down defense game, where your “hero” a frog wielding a pitchfork would defend his swamp from attacking knife hurling goblins. 

This project demonstrates game building concepts in Pygame Graphics, such as 

Use of pygame in creating and importing different class and sprite types
Appearance of user / key controlled player class. 
Appearance of pygame controlled “enemy” class programs to roam randomly until coming into range of player; at which point to follow and hurdle knifes that direction. 
Utilization of animation frames, essentially giving both character and enemy classes a sense of “life” in movement. 
Utilization of hitboxes, player attacking animation can overlap with class enemy hitboxes, thus eliminating the enemy. 
Creation of map barriers to prevent classes from intersecting and travelling off visible map. 


